# The multi-qubit STABILIZER, the formalism behind quantum error correcting codes such as the toric and surface codes,
# modeled and proved by the kernel. A stabilizer code is defined by a set of commuting Pauli operators called the
# stabilizer generators; the protected codespace is their joint plus-one eigenspace. The structural fact that makes the
# whole formalism work is that any two Pauli operators either COMMUTE or ANTICOMMUTE, never anything in between, and the
# generators of a valid code must all pairwise COMMUTE so that their measurements are simultaneously consistent. For a
# multi-qubit Pauli string, written as a tensor product of single-qubit Paulis, two strings commute exactly when the
# number of qubits on which they anticommute is EVEN, because each anticommuting position contributes one sign flip and
# an even number of flips cancels. This file CONSTRUCTS single-qubit Paulis and two-qubit strings, COMPUTES the
# single-qubit commutation table and the even-parity string rule, and PROVES the laws: commutation is symmetric, the
# identity commutes with everything, every operator commutes with itself, the string rule is symmetric, a concrete
# stabilizer pair (the toric-code vertex and plaquette operators) commutes through an even anticommutation count, and a
# concrete pair with an odd count anticommutes.

load @term/seed/atom/flag
  find flag
  find both
  find either

# The four single-qubit Pauli operators (up to phase), mirroring the Pauli group: the identity, the bit flip (X), the
# bit-and-phase flip (Y), and the phase flip (Z).
form pauli
  case identity
  case bit-flip
  case both-flip
  case phase-flip

# A two-qubit Pauli STRING, a tensor product of one single-qubit Pauli on the first qubit and one on the second. A
# stabilizer generator of a two-qubit code is exactly such a string.
form string
  case pair
    link head, like pauli
    link tail, like pauli

# TEMPLATE: one row of the single-qubit commutation table. For a fixed first operator, give the commutation flag against
# each of identity, bit-flip, both-flip, phase-flip. One fuse line per row keeps the four-by-four table DRY.
tree commute-row
  take row
  take at-identity
  take at-bit-flip
  take at-both-flip
  take at-phase-flip

  hook fuse
    case {row}
      fork case, read b
        case identity
          send back
            make {at-identity}
        case bit-flip
          send back
            make {at-bit-flip}
        case both-flip
          send back
            make {at-both-flip}
        case phase-flip
          send back
            make {at-phase-flip}

# Do two single-qubit Paulis COMMUTE? The identity commutes with every operator, and every operator commutes with
# itself, so the diagonal and the identity row and column are all yes. Two DISTINCT non-identity Paulis anticommute, so
# the remaining off-diagonal entries are no. This is the finite four-by-four table at the heart of the formalism.
task commutes
  take a, like pauli
  take b, like pauli
  like flag
  fork case, read a
    fuse commute-row, identity, yes, yes, yes, yes
    fuse commute-row, bit-flip, yes, yes, no, no
    fuse commute-row, both-flip, yes, no, yes, no
    fuse commute-row, phase-flip, yes, no, no, yes

# The EXCLUSIVE-OR of two commutation flags, yes exactly when they differ. Two strings commute when their per-qubit
# commutation flags AGREE (both commute, an even count of zero anticommuting qubits, or both anticommute, an even count
# of two), and anticommute when the flags differ (one anticommuting qubit, an odd count). Agreement is the negation of
# exclusive-or, computed here directly as a flag equality.
task agree
  take a, like flag
  take b, like flag
  like flag
  fork case, read a
    case yes
      send back
        read b
    case no
      fork case, read b
        case yes
          send back
            make no
        case no
          send back
            make yes

# Read the head (first-qubit) Pauli of a string.
task head-of
  take s, like string
  like pauli
  fork case, read s
    case pair
      link head
      link tail
      send back
        read head

# Read the tail (second-qubit) Pauli of a string.
task tail-of
  take s, like string
  like pauli
  fork case, read s
    case pair
      link head
      link tail
      send back
        read tail

# Do two two-qubit Pauli STRINGS commute? Compute the single-qubit commutation flag on the head qubit and on the tail
# qubit, then take their agreement: the strings commute exactly when the two per-qubit flags agree, which is the
# even-anticommutation rule for two qubits (zero or two anticommuting positions).
task strings-commute
  take p, like string
  take q, like string
  like flag
  send back
    call agree
      call commutes
        call head-of
          read p
        call head-of
          read q
      call commutes
        call tail-of
          read p
        call tail-of
          read q

# SINGLE-QUBIT COMMUTATION IS SYMMETRIC: commutes a b = commutes b a, for every pair of operators. Commutation is a
# relation, not an ordered operation, so the table is symmetric across its diagonal. Proved over all sixteen pairs.
rule commutes-is-symmetric
  seat a, like pauli
  seat b, like pauli
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call commutes
        read a
        read b
      call commutes
        read b
        read a
  fold a, b

# THE IDENTITY COMMUTES WITH EVERY PAULI: commutes identity p = yes, for every operator. The identity is in the center
# of the Pauli group, so it never obstructs a stabilizer measurement. Proved over all four operators.
rule identity-commutes-with-everything
  seat p, like pauli
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call commutes
        make identity
        read p
      make yes
  fold p

# EVERY PAULI COMMUTES WITH ITSELF: commutes p p = yes, for every operator. The diagonal of the table is all yes, which
# is why a single stabilizer generator is always consistent with itself. Proved over all four operators.
rule every-pauli-commutes-with-itself
  seat p, like pauli
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call commutes
        read p
        read p
      make yes
  fold p

# THE STRING COMMUTATION RULE IS SYMMETRIC: strings-commute p q = strings-commute q p, over all two-qubit strings. The
# proof ranges over the two strings, that is over four single-qubit Paulis, sixteen times sixteen pairs in all, and
# follows from the symmetry of the single-qubit table together with the symmetry of agreement.
rule strings-commute-is-symmetric
  seat a, like pauli
  seat b, like pauli
  seat c, like pauli
  seat d, like pauli
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call strings-commute
        make pair
          bind head
            read a
          bind tail
            read b
        make pair
          bind head
            read c
          bind tail
            read d
      call strings-commute
        make pair
          bind head
            read c
          bind tail
            read d
        make pair
          bind head
            read a
          bind tail
            read b
  fold a, b, c, d

# A CONCRETE STABILIZER PAIR COMMUTES: the string (bit-flip, bit-flip), the toric-code vertex operator X-X, and the
# string (phase-flip, phase-flip), the plaquette operator Z-Z, COMMUTE. They anticommute on BOTH qubits (bit-flip and
# phase-flip anticommute), an EVEN count of two, so the sign flips cancel and the strings commute. This even overlap is
# exactly why the vertex and plaquette stabilizers of the toric code can be measured together and the code is
# consistent. Proved by direct computation.
rule toric-vertex-and-plaquette-commute
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call strings-commute
        make pair
          bind head
            make bit-flip
          bind tail
            make bit-flip
        make pair
          bind head
            make phase-flip
          bind tail
            make phase-flip
      make yes
  calm hold

# A CONCRETE ANTICOMMUTING PAIR: the string (bit-flip, identity) and the string (phase-flip, identity) ANTICOMMUTE. They
# anticommute on the first qubit and commute (through the identity) on the second, an ODD count of one, so a single
# uncanceled sign flip remains. A pair like this can never both belong to the stabilizer of a valid code; an odd overlap
# is precisely the forbidden case. Proved by exhibiting strings-commute as no.
rule odd-overlap-pair-anticommutes
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call strings-commute
        make pair
          bind head
            make bit-flip
          bind tail
            make identity
        make pair
          bind head
            make phase-flip
          bind tail
            make identity
      make no
  calm hold
